  "heat": 2,
  "text": "Paul opens the last chapter of Romans by telling an entire church to give this woman whatever she asks for. Romans sixteen. The final chapter. And he starts it with a personal commendation. \"I commend unto you Phebe our sister, which is a servant of the church which is at Cenchrea.\" Now. That word servant. In Greek it is diakonos. Elsewhere in the New Testament translators render that exact same word deacon. Or minister. Here, historically, a lot of them chose servant. Scholars have gone back and forth on it for centuries, and you should know that is a translation decision, not a settled fact. Then Paul asks the Roman church to receive her, and, \"that ye assist her in whatsoever business she hath need of you.\" Whatever she needs. Give it to her. And then his reason. \"For she hath been a succourer of many, and of myself also.\" A patron. A protector. She had backed Paul personally. One more thing, and I want to be straight with you. You will often hear that Phoebe carried the letter to Rome. That is a very old and very reasonable inference, because she is commended first, right at the front. It is not stated in the text. Inference. Not chapter and verse. What is chapter and verse is that Paul told a whole church to do what she asked.",
